window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-2-full-book"][29] = "<h1>JOURNEY 5: KARYA — Autonomous Email &amp; Calendar Agent for Knowledge Workers</h1>\n<h2>23.5.1 Origin and context</h2>\n<p>This is the most ambitious of the five journeys. Karya is an autonomous AI agent — meaning it doesn't just suggest actions, it takes them. It reads your email, schedules your meetings, drafts replies, books travel, and follows up — all with minimal supervision once trusted.</p>\n<p>Why this is hard:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trust is the binding constraint.</strong> Users won't let an AI take actions on their behalf until they trust it. Trust takes weeks to build, can be destroyed in one bad action.</li>\n<li><strong>The agent has multiple interaction surfaces.</strong> Email, calendar, sometimes Slack, sometimes Notion. Each integration adds complexity and risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Autonomy UX is not solved.</strong> &quot;What do I let the agent do without asking?&quot; has no industry-standard answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Recovery from failure is critical.</strong> When the agent gets it wrong (and it will), what happens?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The market context: by 2026, AI agents have become a popular product category. Cognition Devin (coding agent), Adept ACT-2, Multion, OpenAI's Operator, and several incumbent moves (Microsoft Copilot, Google Duet) have established the pattern. None have nailed personal productivity (email + calendar) for knowledge workers.</p>\n<p>The hypothesis: an agent specifically designed for knowledge-worker email and calendar — with a deliberate trust-building UX and strong defaults — could win this category.</p>\n<h2>23.5.2 Discovery — power-user interviews</h2>\n<p>For this product, the target user is sharp: knowledge workers (PMs, founders, executives, consultants) who deal with 100+ emails/day and 8-15 meetings/day.</p>\n<p>Twenty interviews:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>8 startup founders / CEOs.</li>\n<li>5 senior PMs / VPs at tech companies.</li>\n<li>4 management consultants.</li>\n<li>3 executive assistants (who currently do this job manually).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What we asked:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Walk me through your typical email morning.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;What's your worst recurring email/calendar task?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;If you had a perfect assistant, what would they do?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;What would terrify you about an AI handling your email?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What we learned:</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 1: Inbox triage exhausts cognitive capacity.</strong> &quot;I spend 90 minutes a day deleting, archiving, and triaging email. Most of it is junk. The signal is buried in noise.&quot; (16 of 20.)</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 2: Scheduling back-and-forth is brutal.</strong> &quot;Setting up a 30-min meeting takes 6 emails. Multiply by 15 meetings/week.&quot; (14 of 20.)</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 3: Follow-ups slip.</strong> &quot;I owe 8 people responses. I forget. They get frustrated.&quot; (12 of 20.)</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 4: Drafting routine replies takes time.</strong> Confirmations, polite declines, intro responses. &quot;I'm rewriting the same email 20 times a week.&quot; (15 of 20.)</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 5: Travel booking is friction-heavy.</strong> &quot;Three emails with the executive assistant + browsing Cleartrip + checking calendar. Hours per trip.&quot; (8 of 20, mostly executives.)</p>\n<p><strong>The trust question — most surprising finding:</strong></p>\n<p>Nearly every interviewee, when asked &quot;what would terrify you about AI on your email,&quot; gave the same set of answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Sending an email I would never have sent.&quot; (Tone errors, premature commitments.)</li>\n<li>&quot;Sharing private information.&quot; (Forwarding the wrong thing; replying-all by mistake.)</li>\n<li>&quot;Deleting something I needed.&quot; (Aggressive triage.)</li>\n<li>&quot;Booking a meeting I didn't want.&quot; (Calendar invite to someone the user was avoiding.)</li>\n<li>&quot;Missing something important.&quot; (Filing it as junk by mistake.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are <strong>specific</strong> fears. The agent must address each, individually, in its design.</p>\n<p><strong>What people said they wanted that they probably don't:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;An AI that just handles everything autonomously.&quot; Most users <em>say</em> this; they don't actually want it. They want oversight with leverage. Pure autonomy is too scary at v1.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>JTBD synthesis</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>&quot;When I'm overwhelmed with email and scheduling, I want an AI assistant that handles routine tasks and surfaces only what genuinely needs my attention — without the fear that it will say something wrong, leak private information, or miss something important — so I can spend my cognitive energy on actual work.&quot;</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The job is &quot;reduce cognitive load while preserving control.&quot; Both halves matter.</p>\n<h2>23.5.3 The PRD (compressed)</h2>\n<p><strong>Product:</strong> Karya — Personal AI agent for email and calendar.</p>\n<p><strong>Core capabilities (in order of trust difficulty):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Triage (read-only).</strong> Karya reads email and categorizes: must-respond, can-wait, junk. No actions taken.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Drafts.</strong> Karya drafts replies; user reviews and sends.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Auto-reply for low-risk emails.</strong> Things like &quot;thanks, will do&quot; and meeting confirmations. User pre-approves the categories.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Calendar coordination.</strong> Karya proposes meeting times; user approves. After trust, Karya can confirm directly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Follow-up tracking.</strong> Karya watches for unreplied threads where user committed to respond. Reminds. Drafts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Travel and logistics.</strong> Books flights, hotels per user preferences. Always confirms before payment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Long-term: agentic workflows.</strong> &quot;Find me 3 candidates with these qualifications and schedule intros.&quot; Multi-step autonomous work.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The MVP is capabilities 1-4. Capabilities 5-6 in v1.1. Capability 7 is v2+.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary users:</strong> Knowledge workers receiving 50+ emails/day, with 5+ meetings/day. Initial target: founders, executives, senior managers in tech.</p>\n<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> $30/month consumer; $50/user/month team. Premium tier ($100/month) for executives with higher message volume and travel features.</p>\n<p><strong>Success metrics:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Time saved per user (self-reported and behavioral measures).</li>\n<li>Trust progression (user enables progressively more autonomy).</li>\n<li>Action accuracy (errors per 1000 actions taken).</li>\n<li>Net Promoter Score.</li>\n<li>Conversion: free trial → paid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-goals:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>We are not a productivity OS. We don't replace Notion, Asana.</li>\n<li>We are not a CRM. We don't track customer relationships systematically.</li>\n<li>We are not a calendaring app. We work with Google Calendar / Outlook; we don't replace them.</li>\n<li>We are not a writing assistant. We help with email drafts; we're not a Grammarly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.5.4 The trust progression UX — the most important design decision</h2>\n<p>The single biggest design choice: how does the agent earn autonomy?</p>\n<p><strong>The model: progressive trust through demonstrated reliability.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phase 0 (week 1): Observation.</strong>\nKarya does nothing. It reads the user's email and calendar to learn patterns. Shows insights: &quot;I noticed you respond to investor emails within 4 hours, internal team within 24 hours.&quot; Builds user understanding of what Karya sees.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 1 (weeks 2-4): Suggestions only.</strong>\nKarya suggests actions but doesn't take any. &quot;Here's a draft reply to John's email.&quot; User reviews; sends or rejects. Karya learns from acceptance/rejection patterns.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 2 (weeks 5-8): Auto-actions for low-risk categories.</strong>\nUser explicitly enables specific categories for autonomy: &quot;auto-confirm meeting requests from my team,&quot; &quot;auto-archive newsletters,&quot; &quot;auto-decline cold outreach.&quot; Karya does these without asking; user audits.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 3 (weeks 9+): Expanded autonomy.</strong>\nUser progressively enables more categories. &quot;Schedule meetings up to 30 minutes with my team without asking.&quot; &quot;Send routine status updates.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 4 (months 3+): Full agentic.</strong>\nUser trusts Karya enough that the dashboard shows: &quot;Today Karya handled 23 emails, scheduled 5 meetings, sent 3 follow-ups. Here's the audit log.&quot;</p>\n<p>The progression takes time — typically 2-3 months for full trust. We don't rush it.</p>\n<h3>The boundary system</h3>\n<p>Within each phase, the user has explicit boundary controls:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>People allowlists/blocklists.</strong> &quot;Always ask before responding to my CEO.&quot; &quot;Never auto-respond to my therapist.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Topic boundaries.</strong> &quot;Don't auto-respond about pricing.&quot; &quot;Don't book meetings about [sensitive topic].&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Action boundaries.</strong> &quot;Never delete email.&quot; &quot;Always show me drafts before sending if amount &gt; $1000.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Time-of-day boundaries.</strong> &quot;Don't take any actions after 8pm.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Confirmation thresholds.</strong> &quot;Confirm before any action involving more than 5 people.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The boundary system is the reassurance that prevents the user's worst-case fears.</p>\n<h3>The audit log</h3>\n<p>Every action Karya takes is logged. The audit log is searchable, filterable, and prominent. Users can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>See exactly what Karya did and when.</li>\n<li>Undo actions (within a window — replies that haven't been read can be unsent; meetings can be uncreated).</li>\n<li>Provide feedback: &quot;This action was wrong&quot; — Karya learns.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The audit log is not buried in settings. It's a primary surface.</p>\n<h2>23.5.5 AI architecture</h2>\n<p><strong>Models:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frontier model for understanding context, drafting nuanced replies, edge cases (Claude Opus or GPT-4o).</li>\n<li>Smaller, faster model for routine triage (Claude Haiku or GPT-4o-mini).</li>\n<li>Embedding model for personal context (relationship history, prior emails).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>RAG over user's data:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Index of all the user's email history (with privacy controls).</li>\n<li>Index of contacts and relationship context.</li>\n<li>Index of past actions and their outcomes (success/correction).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Memory:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long-term: relationship context. &quot;Sara is my co-founder. Casual tone. Respond fast.&quot;</li>\n<li>Medium-term: ongoing threads. &quot;I owe John a response on the Q3 plan.&quot;</li>\n<li>Short-term: today's context. &quot;User is in meetings 2-5pm; flag urgent emails.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Action layer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Defined set of actions (send email, archive, schedule, etc.).</li>\n<li>Each action has structured parameters and validation rules.</li>\n<li>Action execution gated by permission system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Safety layers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-flight checks: before any auto-action, run safety check (is this email to a sensitive contact? Does it commit to something? Does it share private info?).</li>\n<li>Reasoning trace: for complex actions, the agent shows its reasoning so the user can audit.</li>\n<li>Failure recovery: if an action fails or seems suspicious, the agent escalates to user.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Privacy architecture:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All personal data stays in user-isolated storage.</li>\n<li>No training on user data without explicit consent.</li>\n<li>End-to-end encryption for at-rest storage.</li>\n<li>Clear data deletion controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is essential. Email is the most sensitive workplace data. A privacy violation kills the company.</p>\n<h2>23.5.6 The metrics tree</h2>\n<pre><code>                    Active subscribers\n                            |\n              ______________|_____________\n             |                            |\n        New paying users           Retention\n             |                            |\n       ______|______             __________|_________\n      |             |           |                    |\n   Trial-to-paid  Trust       Trust              Quality\n   conversion    progression  level             of actions\n                                |                    |\n                          _____|_____         _____|_____\n                         |           |       |           |\n                      Time-to-     Auto-   Action      User\n                      first-      action  errors      corrections\n                      autonomy   count    per 1000    per 100\n                      (days)     /week    actions     actions\n</code></pre>\n<p>The killer metric: <strong>action error rate per 1000 actions</strong>. Below 1: trust grows. Above 5: trust collapses.</p>\n<p>The trust progression metric: <strong>% of users at Phase 3 by week 8</strong>. Tells us if our progression model is working.</p>\n<h2>23.5.7 The MVP plan</h2>\n<p><strong>Phase 1 (months 1-4): Trust-building MVP</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capabilities 1-3 (triage, drafts, auto-reply for low-risk).</li>\n<li>Email integration (Gmail first; Outlook in v1.1).</li>\n<li>Closed alpha with 50 power users (founders, senior PMs).</li>\n<li>Heavy white-glove onboarding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Phase 2 (months 5-8): Calendar + follow-up</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capability 4 (calendar coordination).</li>\n<li>Capability 5 (follow-up tracking).</li>\n<li>Public beta. 1000 users target.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Phase 3 (months 9-12): Travel + scale</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capability 6 (travel booking).</li>\n<li>Self-serve onboarding.</li>\n<li>10,000 user target.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Kill criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Action error rate above 2% sustained: kill or rebuild safety layers.</li>\n<li>User trust progression: &lt;40% reach Phase 2 by week 6: UX or messaging issue; investigate.</li>\n<li>Conversion (trial → paid) below 12%: pricing or value-prop issue.</li>\n<li>Negative incident with PR coverage: pause feature, investigate, transparent communication.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.5.8 Trade-offs accepted</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Slow trust &gt; fast value.</strong> We don't rush the user into autonomy. Slower perceived value early; more durable retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Frontier model &gt; cheaper alternatives.</strong> Email tone errors are intolerable. We pay for quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Read access default; write access progressive.</strong> Karya can read everything from day one (after permission); writing actions are gated. Trust asymmetry intentional.</li>\n<li><strong>Audit log prominent &gt; buried.</strong> The &quot;what did Karya do today&quot; surface is primary. Users need it.</li>\n<li><strong>Power-user focus &gt; broad consumer.</strong> Power users have the volume that makes the value proposition clear. We win them first.</li>\n<li><strong>Gmail first &gt; all-or-nothing.</strong> Half the addressable market starts; faster ship; pure scope discipline.</li>\n<li><strong>Subscription &gt; usage-based pricing.</strong> Subscriptions align our incentives with reduced cognitive load (not more agent activity).</li>\n<li><strong>Personal product &gt; team product at MVP.</strong> B2B team plans are v2. Solo users first.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>23.5.9 Post-launch retrospective</h2>\n<p><strong>Year 1, looking back:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What worked:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trust progression model was right. Users who completed Phase 0-2 retained at 80%+. Users who skipped Phase 0 (impatient) churned at 60%.</li>\n<li>Audit log as primary surface was a hit. Users referenced it frequently.</li>\n<li>Triage accuracy reached 94% within 2 months — thanks to reasoning trace + correction loop.</li>\n<li>Word of mouth among founders/PMs. The product spoke to a specific power-user pain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What didn't:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calendar coordination was harder than expected. Multi-party scheduling has subtle politics (&quot;don't schedule with Sara before Tom because Tom should hear first&quot;). Couldn't fully automate; required user oversight longer than planned.</li>\n<li>Drafting tone — quality varied dramatically by user. Highly individual. Required heavy personalization, slowed time to value.</li>\n<li>Some users wanted full autonomy from day one and were frustrated by slow progression. Lost some.</li>\n<li>Errors are public when they happen. One viral negative tweet about Karya sending an inappropriate email cost us 3 weeks of trust.</li>\n<li>B2B demand was higher than expected. Team plans launched in v1.2, earlier than planned.</li>\n<li>Travel feature shipped late and was used less than expected. Most power users had EAs already; Karya wasn't the marginal value here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What we changed in v1.x:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Added &quot;express trust&quot; mode for users who explicitly request faster autonomy with clear acceptance of risk.</li>\n<li>Major tone personalization investment in v1.2.</li>\n<li>Incident response runbook for any error that goes public.</li>\n<li>B2B team plans launched in v1.2.</li>\n<li>De-emphasized travel; redirected resources to email/calendar excellence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What we'd do differently:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>More user segmentation upfront. Founders need different things than ICs at companies.</li>\n<li>Tone personalization is core, not nice-to-have. Build it from day one.</li>\n<li>Public error response process from the beginning. We were caught flat-footed.</li>\n<li>B2B GTM earlier. The team product is more valuable than solo.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.5.10 What would Google look for here?</h2>\n<p>Agentic AI is the frontier of product thinking. Strong signals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trust-building as the central design pattern.</strong> Most agent products fail here.</li>\n<li><strong>Autonomy progression UX.</strong> Phase 0-4 is structurally smart.</li>\n<li><strong>Boundary system thoroughness.</strong> People, topic, action, time, threshold boundaries.</li>\n<li><strong>Audit log as primary surface.</strong> Counterintuitive design choice; right one.</li>\n<li><strong>Specific user fears mapped to specific defenses.</strong> Discovery → architecture → safety layers.</li>\n<li><strong>Honest about quality variance and tone challenges.</strong> Not over-promising AI.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Likely follow-ups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;What's your moat 18 months out as Microsoft, Google, OpenAI launch competing agents?&quot; (Strategic competition.)</li>\n<li>&quot;Walk me through how you'd handle a major public incident — Karya sends a bad email that goes viral.&quot; (Crisis management.)</li>\n<li>&quot;How do you think about the line between agent that proposes vs agent that acts?&quot; (Agentic philosophy.)</li>\n<li>&quot;What's your view on B2B vs B2C as the primary path for personal AI agents?&quot; (Strategic direction.)</li>\n<li>&quot;How do you know if your trust progression is too fast or too slow for a given user?&quot; (Personalization sophistication.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A strong PM has thought through each. Agentic products are where 2026-2027 PM thinking is being forged. If you can speak fluently about Karya's design — even though it's hypothetical — you're operating at the edge of the discipline.</p>\n<h2>23.5.11 Closing — what these five journeys teach</h2>\n<p>Five different products. Five different domains. Five different sets of decisions. The patterns recur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Discovery rigor wins.</strong> All five started with deep user research before building.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade-offs are explicit.</strong> Each journey named 8 specific trade-offs accepted, not avoided.</li>\n<li><strong>Metrics trees discipline thinking.</strong> Each journey decomposed success into actionable inputs.</li>\n<li><strong>Kill criteria define commitment.</strong> Each journey said: here's what would make us stop.</li>\n<li><strong>Retrospectives are honest.</strong> Each journey named what didn't work, not just what did.</li>\n<li><strong>AI is deliberate.</strong> Each journey has a clear &quot;where AI sits, where it doesn't&quot; architecture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are not the only patterns. They are necessary ones. PMs who can produce work at this depth — for any product, in any domain — operate at Senior PM level and above.</p>\n<p>If you've absorbed these journeys, you have five complete case studies you can draw from in interviews. You have five sets of design decisions you can defend. You have five worked examples of how to think.</p>\n<p>The next chapter, Part 24, is the interview gauntlet. Use these journeys as your raw material.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>End of Part 23.</em></p>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n";
